8. AUTOMATED DEFENSIVE REMEDIATION PLAYBOOKS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Every discovered vulnerability node in ReconFlow attaches an actionable, 
technical playbook to eliminate the vulnerability:

A. Exposed Environment Files (.env, .sql, .yaml, .conf):
• NGINX Directive:
    location ~ /\.(?!well-known) { deny all; return 404; }
• Apache Directive:
    <FilesMatch "^\.">
      Require all denied
    </FilesMatch>
• Key Rotation: Immediately invalidate and rotate all database passwords, 
  API keys, and secret tokens exposed in the file.
• Search Console Removal: Issue an expedited URL de-indexing request through 
  Google Search Console.

B. Public API Documentation Portals (Swagger UI, GraphQL Playground):
• Access Barrier: Place /swagger-ui, /docs, and /graphiql routes behind 
  corporate SSO, an identity-aware proxy, or HTTP Basic Authentication.
• Schema Hardening: Disable GraphQL schema introspection in production builds.
• Header Injection: Add 'X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow' HTTP header to all 
  documentation routes.

C. Public Code Leaks (GitHub):
• Secret Revocation: Invalidate the leaked credentials in the cloud console.
• Git History Purge: Use git-filter-repo to rewrite repository commit history:
    git filter-repo --invert-paths --path <leaked-file>
• Workstation Prevention: Enforce pre-commit hooks (Gitleaks, TruffleHog).

D. Misconfigured Public Cloud Storage (AWS S3):
• Bucket Access Block:
    aws s3api put-public-access-block --bucket <bucket-name> \
      --public-access-block-configuration "BlockPublicAcls=true,IgnorePublicAcls=true,BlockPublicPolicy=true,RestrictPublicBuckets=true"
• Presigned URLs: Shift file downloads to short-lived pre-signed URLs.


